"What is the definition of whole body oxygen delivery, and how is it calculated?","<bos><start_of_turn>user
What is the definition of whole body oxygen delivery, and how is it calculated?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","Whole blood oxygen delivery (VO) is the amount of oxygen that is transported by the blood to the tissues of the body per unit time. It is an important indicator of the body's ability to transport oxygen and is influenced by the availability of oxygen in the air, the efficiency of the lungs to absorb oxygen, and the efficiency of the blood to transport oxygen.

To calculate VO, the following equation is used:

VO = VOO × V̇
where:

VO = Volume of oxygen delivered per unit time (e.g., ml/min)
VOO = Volume of oxygen in the blood per unit time (e.g., ml/min)
V̇ = Volume of blood per unit time (e.g., min)

VOO is calculated as:

VOO = VO × α
where:

VO = Volume of oxygen in the inspired air per unit time (e.g., ml/min)
α = Oxygen extraction efficiency (the fraction of oxygen that is absorbed by the lungs)

V̇ is the blood flow rate, which can be calculated using the following equation:

V̇ = V̇_I - V̇_D
where:

V̇_I = Volume of blood entering the tissues per unit time (e.g., ml/min)
V̇_D = Volume of blood leaving the tissues per unit time (e.g., ml/min)

In summary, the calculation of VO involves considering the volume of oxygen in the inspired air, the efficiency of oxygen absorption by the lungs, and the blood flow rate to the tissues.
