<USER_REQUEST>
What were the key findings of the GELA LNH-93-1 study comparing aggressive chemotherapy (ACVBP followed by consolidation) versus standard chemotherapy (CHOP) plus radiotherapy (RT) in patients ≤60 years old with low-risk, stages I–II intermediate-risk non-Hodgkin lymphoma (NHL)?
</USER_REQUEST>

<GROUND_TRUTH>
In the **GELA LNH-93-1** study, 647 patients ≤60 years old with low-risk (IPI 0), stages I–II intermediate-risk NHL (including extranodal or bulky disease) were randomized to aggressive chemotherapy (ACVBP followed by consolidation) or standard chemotherapy (CHOP) plus radiotherapy (RT). The aggressive chemotherapy arm (ACVBP) showed superior 5-year event-free survival (82% vs. 74%) and overall survival (90% vs. 81%) compared to CHOP + RT, regardless of bulky disease. However, ACVBP was associated with high toxicity (e.g., secondary malignancies), limiting its clinical use.  

$\boxed{\text{ACVBP improved EFS and OS but was too toxic for routine use.}}$
</GROUND_TRUTH>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The GELF LNH-93-1 study compares two treatment methods for low-risk, stages I–II intermediate-risk non-Hodgdon lymphoma (NHL) in patients ≤60 years:  
1. **Aggressive chemotherapy (ACVBP followed by consolidation)**  
2. **Standard chemotherapy (CHOP) plus radiotherapy (RT)**.  

The key findings of the study are:  
- **No significant difference in 5-year survival** between the two groups.  
- **Acute-dose-adjusted ACVBP followed by consolidation resulted in a higher risk of gastrointestinal and gastrointestinal-related-intestinal side effects** compared to CHOP + RT.  
- **CHOP + RT resulted in a higher risk of gastrointestinal and gastrointestinal-related-intestinal side effects** compared to ACVBP followed by consolidation.  

Thus, the study suggests that for low-risk, stages I–II intermediate-risk NHL in patients ≤60 years, CHOP + RT is a safer option with similar 5-year survival to aggressive chemotherapy.  

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
&\text{No significant difference in 5-year survival.} \\
&\text{ACVBP followed by consolidation has higher GI/GI-related GI SE.} \\
&\text{CHOP + RT has higher GI/GI-related IG SE.} \\
&\text{CHOP + RT is safer with similar 5-yr survival.}
\end{aligned}
}

