","Isotopologues and isotopomers are two closely related terms in chemistry that describe variations in the chemical structure due to isotopic substitution. Here’s the key difference:

1. **Isotopologues**: Isotopologues are compounds that have the same molecular formula but differ in the isotopic composition of their atoms. For example, \(^{12}\text{C}_2\text{H}_6\) (ethane) and \(^{13}\text{C}_2\text{H}_6\) (isotopically labeled ethane) are isotopologues because they have the same molecular formula but differ in the carbon isotope (12C vs. 13C).

2. **Isotomers**: Isotomers are compounds that have the same molecular formula and the same isotopic composition, but differ in the connectivity of atoms. For example, \(^{12}\text{C}_2\text{H}_6\) (ethane) and \(^{12}\text{C}_2\text{H}_5\text{OH}\) (ethanol) are not isotopologues because they have different structures (ethane is a alkane, while ethanol is an alcohol).

Thus, isotopologues are defined by isotopic substitution, while isotomers are defined by structural differences.
