","When solid benzoic acid (H(C₇H₅O₂)) reacts with sodium hydroxide (NaOH), the observable effects are:
1. The solid benzoic acid dissolves in the solution.
2. The solution becomes basic (alkaline) due to the formation of sodium benzoate (Na(C₇H₅O₂)) and water.
3. The solution may also become slightly blue if phenolphthalein or another suitable indicator is added.

The fully ionized equation for the reaction is:
\[
\text{H(C}_7\text{H}_5\text{O}_2 + \text{NaOH} \rightarrow \text{Na(C}_7\text{H}_5\text{O}_2 + \text{H}_2\text{O}
\]
This shows the dissociation of benzoic acid (H(C₇H₅O₂)) into its ions (H⁺ and C₇H₅O₂⁻) and the reaction of the H⁺ ion with NaOH to form water (H₂O) and sodium benzoate (Na(C₇H₅O₂)). 

Final answer: $\boxed{\text{H(C}_7\text{H}_5\text{O}_2 + \text{NaOH} \rightarrow \text{Na(C}_7\text{H}_5\text{O}_2 + \text{H}_2\text{O}}$
