<start_of_turn>model
","The key difference between σ (sigma) and π (pi) molecular orbitals lies in the electron density distribution:  
- **σ molecular orbitals** have electron density concentrated along the axis between the nuclei of the bonded atoms. This is a head-to-head overlap of atomic orbitals, resulting in a symmetrical electron distribution.  
- **π molecular orbitals** have electron density concentrated above and below the axis between the nuclei of the bonded atoms. This is a side-by-side overlap of atomic orbitals, resulting in a directional electron distribution.  

Thus, σ orbitals are formed by head-by-head overlap, while π orbitals are formed by side-by-side overlap.  

\boxed{\text{σ: Head-to-head overlap, electron density along the axis}} \\ \boxed{\text{π: Side-by-side overlap, electron density above/below the axis}}
