<start_of_turn>model
","The molecular formula C4H10O can exhibit two types of isomerism: structural isomerism and optical isomerism.

1. Structural isomerism: This occurs when the molecules have the same molecular formula but different arrangements of atoms in their structure. For C4H10O, there are two types of structural isomerism:

a) Chain isomerism: This occurs when the carbon skeleton of the molecule is different. For C4H10O, chain isomerism can result in the formation of 1-butanol and 2-butanol.

b) Functional group isomerism: This occurs when the molecules have the same number of atoms but different functional groups. For C4H10O, this can result in the formation of butan-2-one (an ketone) and butan-1-ol (an alcohol).

2. Optical isomerism: This occurs when a molecule has a chiral center, which is a carbon atom bonded to four different groups. In C4H10O, the molecule 2-butanol has a chiral center at the second carbon atom, and thus exhibits optical isomerism. The two isomers are called enantiomers and are non-superimposable mirror images of each other.

In summary, the possible isomers for C4H10O are:

1. 1-butanol
2. 2-butanol
3. Butan-2-one
4. Butan-1-ol

1-butanol and butan-1-ol are structural isomers (chain isomers), while 2-butanol exhibits optical isomerism. Butan-2-one and butan-1-ol are structural isomers (functional group isomers).
