","Absolute asymmetric synthesis is a method in organic chemistry that involves the synthesis of a single enantiomer of a chiral molecule with 100% enantiomeric excess. This means that the reaction produces only one enantiomer of the product, with no contamination from the other enantiomer. This is achieved by using chiral catalysts or reagents that selectively interact with one enantiomer of the substrate, leading to the formation of only one enantiomer of the product.

The difference between absolute asymmetric synthesis and other types of asymmetric synthesis lies in the level of enantioselectivity and the use of a reference system. In other types of asymmetric synthesis, such as enantioselective synthesis, the reaction may produce a high enantiomeric excess (e.g., 90% or 95%), but it does not necessarily produce 100% of the desired enantiomer. In these cases, the enantiomeric excess is determined relative to a reference compound, such as a known enantiomer of the same molecule.

In contrast, in absolute asymmetric synthesis, the enantiomeric excess is determined by comparing the product to a known enantiomer of the same molecule, which is known to be 100% enantiomerically pure. This allows for the absolute determination of the absolute configuration of the product.

An example of absolute asymmetric synthesis is the synthesis of the chiral auxiliary **1-phenylethanol** using the chiral catalyst **Ru(II)-BINAP**. The reaction between a racemic mixture of 1-phenylethanol and a chiral auxiliary forms a chiral intermediate, which can be resolved to give the pure enantiomer of 1-phenylethanol. The absolute configuration of the product can be determined by comparing it to a known enantiomer of 1-phenylethanol.

In summary, absolute asymmetric synthesis is a highly selective method for the synthesis of chiral molecules, which provides the absolute configuration of the product. This is achieved by using chiral catalysts or reagents that selectively interact with one enantiomer of the substrate, leading to the formation of only one enantiomer of the product.
