A water pump designed to deliver 10 liters of water per second is experiencing failure due to cavitation. Determine the minimum inlet pressure required to prevent cavitation from occurring inside the pump. Assume the temperature of the water to be 20°C and that the inlet pipe of the pump is located at a depth of 5 meters below the water surface.
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To determine the minimum inlet pressure required to prevent cavitation, we use the **vapor pressure of the fluid** at the given temperature.

1. **Vapor Pressure of Water at 20°C**:
   At 20°C, the vapor pressure of water is approximately 101.325 kPa.

2. **Pressure Due to Atmospheric Pressure**:
   The pressure at the inlet pipe is also influenced by the atmospheric pressure, which is also approximately 101.325 kPa.

3. **Pressure Due to Head**:
   The water level at the inlet pipe is 5 meters below the surface, so there is an additional pressure due to the hydrostatic pressure of the water.
   \[
   P_{\text{hydro}} = \rho g h = 1000 \, \text{kg/m}^3 \times 9.81 \, \text{m/s}^2 \times 5 \, \text{m} = 49,050 \, \text{Pa} = 49.05 \, \text{kPa}
   \]

4. **Minimum Inlet Pressure**:
   The minimum inlet pressure required to prevent cavitation is the sum of the atmospheric pressure, the hydrostatic pressure, and the vapor pressure of water.
   \[
   P_{\text{inlet}} = P_{\text{atmos}} + P_{\text{hydro}} + P_{\text{vapor}} = 101.325 \, \text{kPa} + 49.05 \, \text{kPa} + 101.325 \, \text{kPa} = 251.67 \, \text{kPa}
   \]

Thus, the minimum inlet pressure required to prevent cavitation is \(\boxed{251.67 \, \text{kPa}}\).

</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

