","To find the maximum percentage change in the wavelength of a photon scattered by an electron, we use the Compton effect formula:

\[
\Delta \lambda = \frac{2\pi \hbar}{m_e c} (1 - \cos \theta)
\]

where:
- \(\Delta \lambda\) is the change in wavelength,
- \(\hbar\) is the reduced Planck constant (\(1.054 \times 10^{-34} \, \text{J s}\)),
- \(m_e\) is the electron mass (\(9.11 \times 10^{-31} \, \text{kg}\)),
- \(c\) is the speed of light (\(3 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s}\)),
- \(\theta\) is the scattering angle.

The maximum wavelength change occurs when \(\theta = 180^\circ\) (i.e., the photon is scattered head-on), so \(\cos \theta = -1\). Substituting \(\theta = 180^\circ\) into the formula:

\[
\Delta \lambda_{\text{max}} = \frac{2\pi \hbar}{m_e c} (1 - (-1)) = \frac{4\pi \hbar}{m_e c}
\]

The maximum percentage change is:

\[
\text{Percentage change} = \left( \frac{\Delta \lambda_{\text{max}}}{\lambda_0} \right) \times 100
\]

where \(\lambda_0 = 2.00 \, \text{Å} = 2.00 \times 10^{-10} \, \text{m}\).

Substituting the values:

\[
\text{Percentage change} = \left( \frac{4\pi \times 1.054 \times 10^{-34}}{9.11 \times 10^{-31} \times 3 \times 10^8} \right) \times \left( \frac{1}{2.00 \times 10^{-10}} \right) \times 100
\]

\[
\text{Percentage change} \approx 0.00028 \, \text{or} \, 0.028\%
\]

Thus, the maximum percentage change in the wavelength of the 2.00 Å photon scattered by an electron is \(\boxed{0.028\%}\).
