A proton with mass \(1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}\) and charge \(1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C}\) accelerates from rest in a uniform electric field of strength \(500 \, \text{N/C}\).  
(a) What is the magnitude of the acceleration of the proton?  
(b) How long does it take the proton to reach a speed of \(35,000 \, \text{m/s}\)?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","(a) The electric field exerts a force on the proton, which leads to acceleration. The force \(F\) is given by \(F = qE\), where \(q\) is the charge of the proton and \(E\) is the electric field strength.  

Using Newton's second law, \(F = ma\), we can solve for the acceleration \(a\):  
\[
a = \frac{F}{m} = \frac{qE}{m} = \frac{(1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C})(500 \, \text{N/C})}{1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}} = 3.99 \times 10^5 \, \text{m/s}^2.
\]  
Thus, the magnitude of the acceleration is \(\boxed{3.99 \times 10^5 \, \text{m/s}^2}\).  

(b) To find the time \(t\) required for the proton to reach a speed \(v = 35,000 \, \text{m/s}\), we use the kinematic equation:  
\[
v = u + at,
\]  
where \(u = 0\) (since the proton starts from rest). Solving for \(t\):  
\[
t = \frac{v - u}{a} = \frac{35,000 \, \text{m/s}}{3.99 \times 10^5 \, \text{m/s}^2} = 0.0877 \, \text{s}.
\]  
Thus, the time required is \(\boxed{0.0877 \, \text{s}}\).
