</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To solve this problem, we use the time dilation formula:

\[
t = \frac{t_0}{\sqrt{1 - \left(\frac{v}{c}\right)^2}}
\]

where:
- \( t \) is the time measured by the stationary observer (Earth),
- \( t_0 \) is the time measured by the moving observer (the cockpit clock),
- \( v \) is the speed of the moving observer (0.6c),
- \( c \) is the speed of light.

Given:
- \( t_0 = 3 \) years (time on the cockpit clock),
- \( v = 0.6c \).

Substitute the values into the formula:

\[
t = \frac{3}{\sqrt{1 - (0.6)^2}} = \frac{3}{\sqrt{1 - 0.36}} = \frac{3}{\sqrt{0.64}} = \frac{3}{0.8} = 3.75 \text{ years}
\]

Thus, the time shown on the cardoor bay clock (since it is moving at the same speed) will also be 3.75 years. The time shown on the Earth clock will be 3.75 years.

**Final Answer**:  
The time shown on the cardoor bay clock is \(\boxed{3.75 \text{ years}}\). The time shown on the Earth clock is \(\boxed{3.75 \text{ years}}\).

