","The net chemical reaction during the discharge of a lead storage battery is:
\[ 2\text{H}_2\text{SO}_4 + \text{Pb(s)} + \text{PbO}_2(\text{s}) \rightarrow \text{PbSO}_4(\text{s}) + \text{PbSO}_4(\text{s}) + 2\text{H}_2\text{O(l)} \]

This reaction can be broken down into two half-reactions:
1. **Oxidation at the anode (Pb electrode):**
  \[ \text{Pb(s)} + \text{H}_2\text{SO}_4(\text{aq}) \rightarrow \text{PbSO}_4(\text{s}) + 2\text{H}^+(\text{aq}) + 2e^- \]
2. **Reduction at the cathode (PbO₂ electrode):**
  \[ \text{PbO}_2(\text{s}) + 2\text{H}^+(\text{aq}) + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{PbSO}_4(\text{s}) + \text{H}_2\text{O(l)} \]

These two half-reactions are combined to give the net reaction.
